BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Santa Rosa Junior College
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
LOCATION:SRJC Multicultural Museum - Santa Rosa Campus
SUMMARY:From Sheep to Loom: Diné Weaving Traditions
DESCRIPTION:For the Diné\, weaving is considered a ceremony. Creating a 
 single blanket requires many steps\, including shearing the sheep\, clean
 ing\, carding\, spinning\, and dyeing the wool\, and then finally weaving
 . It can take hundreds of hours. This exhibit features rugs from the SRJC
  Multicultural Museum's collections\, featuring award winning woven rugs 
 such as a fine "Burntwater" style rug by the great Nellie Roan and many o
 ther examples of Navajo rugs from the 1920s through the 1970s.More inform
 ation available on the SRJC Multicultural Museum website. (https://museum
 .santarosa.edu/)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  For the Diné\, weaving is considered a
  ceremony. Creating a single blanket requires many steps\, including shea
 ring the sheep\, cleaning\, carding\, spinning\, and dyeing the wool\, an
 d then finally weaving. It can take hundreds of hours. This exhibit featu
 res rugs from the SRJC Multicultural Museum's collections\, featuring awa
 rd winning woven rugs such as a fine "Burntwater" style rug by the great 
 Nellie Roan and many other examples of Navajo rugs from the 1920s through
  the 1970s.</p><p><a href="https://museum.santarosa.edu/" target="_blank"
  rel="noopener">More information available on the SRJC Multicultural Muse
 um website.</a></p>
