BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Santa Rosa Junior College
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261114T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261114T180000
LOCATION:Multicultural Museum - Santa Rosa Campus
SUMMARY:Museum Reception -Standing in Her Power: Portraits Beyond the Col
 onial Frame
DESCRIPTION:In early May 2026\, we put out a call for nominations\, askin
 g for Native American women to represent their Nations\, their stories\, 
 and their strength through a powerful portrait exhibition celebrating ide
 ntity\, cultural legacy\, and the resilience of Native women. We received
  far more nominations than we expected. Choosing from so many incredible 
 women whose stories deserve to be told was not easy. Every single entry s
 howed us how beautiful and unique and strong Native American women are.  
 The idea for the Standing in Her Power exhibit was conceived in the Fall 
 of 2025 by student employee Julian Cook\, who wanted to showcase photos o
 f Native American women wearing regalia. Through Julian's connections in 
 the Bay Area\, the project has grown and led to an exciting collaboration
  with photographer Alexa Treviño. Cook continues to push the exhibit for
 ward through deep conversations with the participants in order to add per
 sonal statements about personal power and growth  to the photographs.This
  project is funded by a grant from the Santa Rosa Junior College Foundati
 on Randolph Newman Cultural Enrichment Endowment.For more information\, v
 isit the SRJC Multicultural Museum website. (https://museum.santarosa.edu
 /event/standing-her-power-portraits-beyond-colonial-frame)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  In early May 2026\, we put out a call f
 or nominations\, asking for Native American women to represent their Nati
 ons\, their stories\, and their strength through a powerful portrait exhi
 bition celebrating identity\, cultural legacy\, and the resilience of Nat
 ive women. We received far more nominations than we expected. Choosing fr
 om so many incredible women whose stories deserve to be told was not easy
 . Every single entry showed us how beautiful and unique and strong Native
  American women are.</p><p>  The idea for the Standing in Her Power exhib
 it was conceived in the Fall of 2025 by student employee Julian Cook\, wh
 o wanted to showcase photos of Native American women wearing regalia. Thr
 ough Julian's connections in the Bay Area\, the project has grown and led
  to an exciting collaboration with photographer Alexa Treviño. Cook cont
 inues to push the exhibit forward through deep conversations with the par
 ticipants in order to add personal statements about personal power and gr
 owth<br />  to the photographs.</p><p><em>This project is funded by a gra
 nt from the Santa Rosa Junior College Foundation Randolph Newman Cultural
  Enrichment Endowment.</em></p><p><a href="https://museum.santarosa.edu/e
 vent/standing-her-power-portraits-beyond-colonial-frame" target="_blank" 
 rel="noopener">For more information\, visit the SRJC Multicultural Museum
  website.</a></p>
